  "title": "Kyoto in Five Days: Temples, Bamboo & Slow Nature",
  "summary": "A culture-and-nature loop through Kyoto for two, built around the city's great temple gardens and forested hillsides. Days are grouped by geography so you walk rather than commute: the southern Inari-Toji axis, the Higashiyama lanes, the northern Zen gardens, the Arashiyama riverside, and finally the quiet eastern hills. Kyoto rewards early starts — the Fushimi torii gates and the bamboo grove are near-empty before 8am and unbearable by 11am. Budget assumes a mid-range machiya-style ryokan or business hotel (roughly ¥500-700 per person per night shared), temple entries at ¥300-500 each, and one sit-down meal plus market or izakaya grazing per day. Wikivoyage's warning holds true: the beauty is in the ring of temples and parks around the modern core, so plan to walk a lot and carry cash for small entries.",
